(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-vignettes"]={"title":"Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 60-year-old man has type 2 diabetes and stable heart failure with reduced ejection fraction on an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. Which drug lowers blood glucose and also reduces heart failure deaths and hospitalizations?","choices":["Dapagliflozin","Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Digoxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin, sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors, were developed for diabetes and reduce mortality and hospitalizations in stable chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with hypertension has had three episodes of calcium oxalate kidney stones. Which diuretic class would treat his blood pressure while also lowering the calcium in his urine?","choices":["A loop diuretic","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","An aldosterone antagonist","A thiazide diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease renal calcium excretion, which helps prevent calcium stones, and they treat hypertension. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is starting digoxin for heart failure. Which symptoms should she be told to report because they may signal too much drug?","choices":["A dry cough and swelling of the lips","Nausea, blurred vision and yellow-green halos","Breast tenderness and irregular periods","Ringing in the ears and hearing loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal upset and visual changes, including yellow-green halos, are early signs of digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man takes lithium for bipolar disorder. His physician adds furosemide for leg edema. Which is the main concern with this combination?","choices":["Faster lithium loss with relapse of mania","Loss of diuretic effect from lithium","Reduced lithium clearance with toxicity","Severe hyperkalemia from potassium retention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics decrease lithium clearance and increase its toxicity, so lithium levels need close attention. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is starting ivabradine for heart failure. Which adverse effect should he be warned about?","choices":["Painful breast enlargement over time","A persistent dry cough at night","Ringing in the ears and hearing loss","New palpitations from atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine increases the risk of atrial fibrillation, and also of symptomatic bradycardia and visual phosphenes. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with symptomatic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction also has atrial fibrillation with a fast ventricular rate. Which heart failure drug has rate-control properties that also suit her?","choices":["Milrinone","Digoxin","Dobutamine","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin raises parasympathetic tone at the atrioventricular node, so it can be used for rate control in atrial fibrillation with heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 67-year-old African American man has newly diagnosed stage 1 hypertension, with no kidney disease, no gout and a normal serum potassium. Which diuretic strategy is preferred for long-term blood pressure control?","choices":["A low-dose thiazide","A loop diuretic","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","A potassium-sparing diuretic alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-dose thiazides are preferred for hypertension, work best in elderly and African American patients, and cause few adverse effects. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with known heart failure presents after a holiday week with breathlessness and leg swelling. He ate heavy, salty meals and ran out of tablets days ago. His blood pressure is normal, his rhythm is regular, he is afebrile, and he has no chest pain. What is the most likely precipitant of his decompensation?","choices":["Uncontrolled hypertension","Missed medicines and diet","A new cardiac arrhythmia","A pulmonary infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Missed medications and a high-sodium diet are the classic precipitants of decompensation, and nothing else is present. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman who has taken a potassium-sparing diuretic combined with hydrochlorothiazide for years develops fatigue and a macrocytic anemia with megaloblastic changes. Which potassium-sparing diuretic is classically linked to this finding?","choices":["Triamterene","Amiloride","Spironolactone","Eplerenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Megaloblastic anemia is the distinctive adverse effect of triamterene. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on high-dose furosemide for heart failure has a raised serum bicarbonate and a blood pH above normal, and he is lightheaded on standing. Which acid-base disturbance is most likely?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss","Respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation","Contraction metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Volume depletion and enhanced hydrogen ion secretion from loop diuretics cause a contraction alkalosis. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with heart failure takes furosemide. Which simple home measurement best detects worsening fluid overload early?","choices":["Checking blood sugar each day","Weighing herself every morning","Counting the number of urinations","Taking her temperature each evening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rapid rise in weight over several days is fluid, not tissue, and signals worsening overload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has hypertension, a persistently low serum potassium and a high aldosterone level, diagnosed as primary aldosteronism. Which diuretic is most appropriate?","choices":["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Acetazolamide","Spironolactone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone blocks the aldosterone receptor and is most effective when aldosterone is high; primary aldosteronism is a listed use. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with advanced heart failure takes spironolactone and reports breast tenderness and enlargement. He wants to keep the benefit of the drug. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to amiloride","Add a thiazide diuretic","Switch to acetazolamide","Switch to eplerenone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eplerenone has less effect on androgen receptors and rarely causes gynecomastia, so it keeps the aldosterone antagonist benefit. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with class III heart failure takes lisinopril, carvedilol, spironolactone and furosemide. Which drug in his regimen relieves symptoms but has not been shown to prolong survival?","choices":["Lisinopril","Carvedilol","Furosemide","Spironolactone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are for symptomatic relief only; there is no evidence that they slow progression or lower mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with heart failure takes digoxin and hydrochlorothiazide. Which laboratory value should be kept above 4.0 mEq/L to lessen the risk of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Serum sodium","Serum potassium","Serum glucose","Serum uric acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides lower potassium, which raises the risk of digitalis toxicity, so the potassium level should stay above 4.0 mEq/L. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman takes furosemide for edema and hypertension. Despite taking it daily, her blood pressure and swelling have worsened over a month. Six weeks ago she started daily ibuprofen for knee arthritis. Which interaction most likely explains the loss of effect?","choices":["Potassium loss making the kidney resistant","Aminoglycoside potentiation of the diuretic","Prostaglandin blockade by ibuprofen","Digitalis lowering the diuretic effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs block prostaglandins and blunt the natriuretic and blood pressure response of loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with acute decompensated heart failure needs short-term intravenous support. Which drug is a selective beta-1 agonist that stimulates the force of contraction more than the rate?","choices":["Dobutamine","Milrinone","Digoxin","Ivabradine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dobutamine is a selective beta-1 agonist given by infusion for short-term stabilization. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with heart failure takes lisinopril and a potassium supplement. His physician adds amiloride. Two weeks later he has weakness and a slow, irregular pulse. Which laboratory abnormality is most likely?","choices":["Low serum potassium","Low serum calcium","High serum potassium","High serum uric acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is the main adverse effect of potassium-sparing diuretics, and it is worse with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and potassium supplements. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with heart failure has electrocardiogram evidence of advanced atrioventricular block and a pulse of 38 beats per minute. Which of his heart failure drugs is contraindicated?","choices":["Digoxin","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advanced atrioventricular block and severe bradycardia are listed contraindications to digoxin, which slows nodal conduction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure starts a combination of valsartan and sacubitril. Which effect of neprilysin inhibition contributes to her benefit?","choices":["Block of the sodium-potassium pump in myocytes","Block of the sodium-glucose transporter in the kidney","Peptide buildup causing vasodilation","Block of the pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neprilysin normally degrades vasoactive peptides, so inhibiting it causes vasodilation, natriuresis and diuresis and inhibits myocardial fibrosis. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with heart failure has two weeks of worsening breathlessness, ankle swelling and bibasilar crackles, and her weight is up several pounds. She already takes an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. Which diuretic class is the mainstay for relieving her fluid overload?","choices":["A loop diuretic","A thiazide diuretic","A potassium-sparing diuretic","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics are the mainstay of heart failure therapy: by cutting sodium and water retention they lower preload and relieve congestion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man takes furosemide and starts lisinopril for heart failure. Which laboratory tests should be followed after starting?","choices":["Serum calcium and thyroid function","Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Platelet count and hemoglobin","Serum potassium and kidney function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors can impair renal function and raise serum potassium, and a loop diuretic is already changing potassium. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with heart failure and edema asks what else he can do besides taking his tablets. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid all physical activity","Limit dietary sodium and fluid intake","Drink extra fluid to flush out the kidneys","Eat extra salt to keep his blood pressure up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Restricting sodium and fluid limits edema and improves functional status in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with heart failure and chronic kidney disease takes digoxin, furosemide, carvedilol and lisinopril. He has two days of nausea and poor appetite and now sees yellow-green halos around lights. His pulse is 48 beats per minute. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Beta blocker toxicity","Digoxin toxicity","Hyperkalemia from lisinopril","Ototoxicity from furosemide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anorexia, nausea, yellow-green halos (xanthopsia) and bradycardia are the classic picture of digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with heart failure and diabetes has had two fungal infections of the urinary tract since a new tablet was added to his regimen. Which drug is most likely responsible?","choices":["Spironolactone","Ivabradine","Dapagliflozin","Carvedilol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors cause the kidneys not to reabsorb glucose, and the sugar in the urine promotes fungal infections, along with a risk of hypotension. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man takes acetazolamide for edema. His urine output was high for the first few days but has settled, and the physician explains that this effect wanes. Which explanation is correct?","choices":["Sodium excretion falls back as the rest of the nephron compensates","The drug is destroyed by the liver within days","Potassium loss gradually blocks the carrier","Bicarbonate reabsorption recovers fully in the proximal tubule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The early rise in sodium and potassium excretion fades to a small effect after three to five days, because bicarbonate depletion limits what the drug can block and the loop, distal tubule and collecting duct take up the sodium. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who lives near sea level plans a rapid ascent to a high-altitude trailhead and has had headache, fatigue and breathlessness on earlier trips. Which diuretic can be used to prevent mountain sickness?","choices":["Furosemide","Spironolactone","Hydrochlorothiazide","Acetazolamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mountain sickness is a listed use of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, which acidify the blood and drive faster breathing. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with heart failure has reduced ejection fraction, normal sinus rhythm and a resting heart rate of 82 beats per minute despite the maximum tolerated beta blocker. Which drug can be added to lower his heart rate and decrease hospitalizations?","choices":["Ivabradine","Diltiazem","Milrinone","Dobutamine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine blocks the pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node, is used when patients are maxed out on beta blockers in sinus rhythm with a rate above 70, and reduces hospitalizations and heart failure death. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a history of gout starts a loop diuretic for ascites and develops a painful, swollen great toe ten days later. Which effect of the diuretic most likely triggered the flare?","choices":["More uric acid made by the liver","Rapid potassium loss from the joint space","Higher calcium levels in the joint fluid","Less uric acid excreted, so plasma levels rise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics decrease uric acid excretion and shrink plasma volume, which concentrates uric acid and can precipitate gout. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
